<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pětka</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1920s</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Pětka, Czech for 'the Five', was the informal committee of the leaders of the five main Czech parties in the Czechoslovak First Republic from 1920: the Agrarians, Social Democrats, National Socialists, People's Party and National Democrats. Meeting outside parliament, it agreed government policy and kept coalitions together, giving the republic stability but drawing criticism as rule by party bosses.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Pětka was founded in September 1920 to guide the weak cabinet of Jan Černý, which lacked a political will of its own, at a time when the leading parties could not form a governing coalition. President Tomáš Masaryk, who believed his people still lacked experience of parliamentary government, backed it rather than rule as a dictator.</p><p>Its main force was Antonín Švehla of the Agrarian Party, prime minister from 1922 to 1929, who invited the leaders of the other four main parties. The first members beside him were Alois Rašín of the National Democrats, Rudolf Bechyně of the Social Democrats, Jiří Stříbrný of the Socialists and Jan Šrámek of the People's Party.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The five met regularly to direct the cabinet and advise the prime minister, on the principle summed up in Švehla's slogan, We have agreed that we will agree. Disputes were settled out of public view, and strict party discipline let each leader control his deputies in the Assembly, so that the Pětka has been called the real government of the country. In September 1921 it appears to have chosen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edvard-benes">Edvard Beneš</a> as prime minister, and after his resignation a year later it chose Švehla.</p><p>It helped contain the hyperinflation that swept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> in 1922 and 1923, and in 1924 it directed the National Assembly to pass a National Insurance Law. It is credited with most of the social reforms of 1918 to 1923, among them the eight-hour day, sickness and unemployment relief and limits on the labour of women and children. As the decade went on, its role was absorbed into the cabinet.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Meant to be brief, the forum was reconvened many times and lasted in some form until the end of the First Republic. It was criticised as unconstitutional and undemocratic, and in 1925 Masaryk himself said that he was not in favour of government by experts, explaining such cabinets by the difficulty of moving from monarchy to democracy.</p><p>It is credited with part of the stability that set <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a> apart in the region: the left never dominated a cabinet and the communists never joined a government. Others argue that reliance on backroom bargains left the country unready for problems that defied compromise, above all the rise of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazism">Nazism</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and its effect on the large and increasingly hostile German minority. When the Pětka dissolved, coalition discipline ended and suppressed quarrels between Czech and Slovak politicians came into the open.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pětka” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Coalitions of the main Czech and Slovak parties, the so-called <b>Pětka</b>, managed government, and German parties joined cabinets from 1926.</blockquote><a href="/empire/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
